Title:
EDR Evasion: Process Injection Without WriteProcessMemory

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- The post discusses a process injection approach designed to avoid the common EDR telemetry and detections associated with `WriteProcessMemory`.  
- It focuses on alternative ways to place or modify code in a remote process while reducing reliance on high-signal Win32 APIs typically monitored for injection chains.  
- The write-up likely walks through an injection flow (target selection, memory allocation/mapping, payload placement, and execution) using lower-noise primitives than classic `VirtualAllocEx` + `WriteProcessMemory` + `CreateRemoteThread`.  
- It is primarily useful for red team operators and offensive security researchers evaluating detection coverage and exploring stealthier tradecraft.  
- It’s interesting because many EDR products heavily weight `WriteProcessMemory` in correlation rules; removing it forces defenders to rely on broader behavioral signals (thread creation, memory protections, section mapping, etc.).

Technical Focus:
- Remote process memory manipulation without `WriteProcessMemory`
- Alternative injection primitives (e.g., section mapping / shared memory)
- Remote thread creation and execution triggers
- EDR telemetry evasion and API-level detection gaps
- Windows process/memory internals (VADs, page protections, mappings)

Use Cases:
- Developing lower-signal process injection PoCs for detection testing
- Red team tradecraft to bypass simplistic “WPM-based” injection rules
- Blue team validation of behavioral detections beyond single-API indicators
- Building and tuning EDR correlation logic for injection chains

Keywords:
EDR evasion, process injection, WriteProcessMemory, VirtualAllocEx, CreateRemoteThread, NtWriteVirtualMemory, NtMapViewOfSection, section mapping, shared section, remote thread, Windows internals, memory protections, VAD, API telemetry, behavioral detection, userland hooks, syscall, injection chain